INFO: Versions data: [{'id': 18, 'teacher_id': 64, 'title': 'QA Lesson 20260929_151749', 'summary': 'QA lesson', 'detailed_answer': None, 'learning_objectives': '', 'focus_area': 'Math', 'grade_level': '7', 'content': '# QA Lesson 20260929_151749\n\n**Source document:** ilovepdf_merged.pdf\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="2">\n<h2>Page 2</h2>\n<p>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment for Grade IX students</p>\n<p>Based on Grade VIII Mathematics</p>\n<p>Time: 30 minutes | Total Marks: 25</p>\n<h4>Instructions</h4>\n<p>Choose the one best answer for each question. Attempt all questions. Each question carries 1 mark. You</p>\n<p>may use rough paper for working.</p>\n<p>Domain A. Number &amp; Operations and Sets</p>\n<p>1. Which pair contains one rational number and one irrational number?</p>\n<p>A. \u221a25and 0.4</p>\n<p>B. \u221a8and \U0001d70b</p>\n<p>C. 0.121212 …and</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>D. \u221a49and \u221a10</p>\n<p>2. The number</p>\n<p>\U0001d7d3. \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6 …</p>\n<h4>is best described as</h4>\n<p>A. a rational terminating decimal</p>\n<p>B. a rational non-terminating recurring decimal</p>\n<p>C. an irrational number</p>\n<p>D. an integer</p>\n<p>3. What is</p>\n<p>\u221a\U0001d7ce. \U0001d7ce\U0001d7ce\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf ?</p>\n<h4>A. 0.9</h4>\n<h4>B. 0.009</h4>\n<h4>C. 0.09</h4>\n<h4>D. 0.081</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="3">\n<h2>Page 3</h2>\n<p>4. Eight workers can complete a job in 15 days. At the same rate, how many days would 12 workers take?</p>\n<p>A. 10 days</p>\n<p>B. 12 days</p>\n<p>C. 18 days</p>\n<p>D. 22.5 days</p>\n<p>5. A shopkeeper marks an item at Rs 4,000 and gives a 10% discount. The item originally cost the</p>\n<p>shopkeeper Rs 3,000.</p>\n<p>What percentage profit does the shopkeeper make on the cost price?</p>\n<h4>A. 15%</h4>\n<h4>B. 16</h4>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>3 %</p>\n<h4>C. 20%</h4>\n<h4>D. 33</h4>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>6. A set \U0001d469 has 4 elements. How many elements are in its power set \U0001d477(\U0001d469)?</p>\n<h4>A. 8</h4>\n<h4>B. 4</h4>\n<h4>C. 32</h4>\n<h4>D. 16</h4>\n<p>7. Which statement is always true?</p>\n<p>A. The product of two irrational numbers is always irrational.</p>\n<p>B. The square root of every positive integer is irrational.</p>\n<p>C. Every non-terminating decimal is irrational.</p>\n<p>D. The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.</p>\n<h4>Domain B. Algebra</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<h3>8. Solve</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>3\U0001d465 + 4 &gt; 10</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A. \U0001d465 &lt; -2</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d465 &gt; -2</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d465 &lt; 2</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d465 &gt; 2</p>\n<h4>9. Simplify</h4>\n<p>3\U0001d465(2\U0001d465 - 5) - 2\U0001d465(\U0001d465 - 4)</p>\n<p>A. 4\U0001d4652 - 23\U0001d465</p>\n<p>B. 8\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>C. 4\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>D. 4\U0001d4652 + 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>10. Which expression is equal to</p>\n<p>(\U0001d7d1\U0001d499 - \U0001d7d0)\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. 9\U0001d4652 - 4</p>\n<p>B. 9\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>C. 9\U0001d4652 - 6\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>D. 6\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>11. Which is the correct factorization of</p>\n<p>\U0001d499\U0001d7d0 + \U0001d499 - \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. (\U0001d465 - 4)(\U0001d465 + 3)</p>\n<p>B. (\U0001d465 + 6)(\U0001d465 - 2)</p>\n<p>C. (\U0001d465 + 12)(\U0001d465 - 1)</p>\n<p>D. (\U0001d465 + 4)(\U0001d465 - 3)</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>12. Which ordered pair satisfies both equations?</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 + \U0001d466 = 9</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 - \U0001d466 = 3</p>\n<h4>A. (6, 3)</h4>\n<h4>B. (3, 6)</h4>\n<h4>C. (7, 2)</h4>\n<h4>D. (5, 4)</h4>\n<h4>13. Simplify</h4>\n<p>(\U0001d44e3\U0001d44f2)(\U0001d44e2\U0001d44f4)</p>\n<p>\U0001d44e\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>A. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f6</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d44e5\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d44e6\U0001d44f9</p>\n<p>14. What is the degree of the polynomial \U0001d7d2\U0001d499\U0001d7d1\U0001d49a\U0001d7d0 - \U0001d7d5\U0001d499\U0001d49a\U0001d7d2 + \U0001d7d1?</p>\n<h4>A. 5</h4>\n<h4>B. 2</h4>\n<h4>C. 4</h4>\n<h4>D. 8</h4>\n<p>15. The arithmetic sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ... continues in the same way. Which expression gives its nth</p>\n<p>term?</p>\n<p>A. 5n + 7</p>\n<p>B. 7n - 2</p>\n<p>C. 5n + 2</p>\n<p>D. 5n - 2</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="6">\n<h2>Page 6</h2>\n<p>16. A straight line passes through the points (-1, 2) and (3, 10). What is the gradient of the line?</p>\n<h3>A. 1/2</h3>\n<h3>B. 4</h3>\n<h4>C. -2</h4>\n<h4>D. 2</h4>\n<h4>Domain C. Measurement &amp; Geometry</h4>\n<p>17. A ladder 15 m long is placed against a wall. If its upper end reaches the height of 12 m along the wall,</p>\n<p>then find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.</p>\n<p>A. 3 m</p>\n<p>B. 6 m</p>\n<p>C. 9 m</p>\n<p>D. 12 m</p>\n<p>18. A cone has a base area of \U0001d7d1\U0001d7ce cm\U0001d7d0and a perpendicular height of 9 cm.</p>\n<p>What is its volume?</p>\n<p>A. 90 cm3</p>\n<p>B. 270 cm3</p>\n<p>C. 30 cm3</p>\n<p>D. 810 cm3</p>\n<p>19. Choose the correct statement.</p>\n<p>A. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are congruent.</p>\n<p>B. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are supplementary.</p>\n<p>C. The diagonals of every rectangle bisect each other at right angles.</p>\n<p>D. If a transversal intersects any two lines, the alternate interior angles are always congruent.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="7">\n<h2>Page 7</h2>\n<p>20. A circle has radius 7 cm. An arc subtends an angle of \U0001d7d7\U0001d7ce\u2218at the centre.</p>\n<p>Using \U0001d70b =</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>7 , what is the length of the arc?</p>\n<p>A. 5.5 cm</p>\n<p>B. 22 cm</p>\n<p>C. 44 cm</p>\n<p>D. 11 cm</p>\n<p>21. In a right-angled triangle, if the opposite side = 4 cm and the hypotenuse = 5 cm, then \U0001d460\U0001d456\U0001d45b \U0001d703 =?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>22. What is the sum of the interior angles of a 7-sided polygon?</p>\n<h4>A. 720\u2218</h4>\n<h4>B. 900\u2218</h4>\n<h4>C. 1080\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. 1260\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. Statistics &amp; Probability</h4>\n<h4>23. Find the median of</h4>\n<p>12, 4, 7, 2, 5</p>\n<h4>A. 6</h4>\n<h4>B. 5</h4>\n<h4>C. 7</h4>\n<h4>D. 10</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="8">\n<h2>Page 8</h2>\n<p>24. A fair coin is tossed and a fair six-sided die is rolled.</p>\n<p>What is the probability of getting a head and an even number?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>25. The scores 2, 4, 6 and 8 occur with frequencies 1, 3, 4 and 2 respectively. What is the mean score?</p>\n<h4>A. 5.0</h4>\n<h4>B. 5.4</h4>\n<h4>C. 6.0</h4>\n<h4>D. 5.6</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="9">\n<h2>Page 9</h2>\n<h3>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment - Answer Key</h3>\n<p>Grade IX | Based on Grade VIII Mathematics | 25 Marks</p>\n<h3>Question Answer Diagnostic Concept</h3>\n<p>1 D Rational and irrational</p>\n<p>numbers</p>\n<p>2 B Recurring decimals</p>\n<p>3 C Square roots of decimals</p>\n<p>4 A Inverse proportion</p>\n<p>5 C Financial arithmetic / profit</p>\n<p>percentage</p>\n<p>6 D Power set</p>\n<p>7 D Properties of rational and</p>\n<p>irrational numbers</p>\n<p>8 A Linear inequality</p>\n<p>9 C Simplification of algebraic</p>\n<p>expressions</p>\n<p>10 B Algebraic identity</p>\n<h4>11 D Factorization</h4>\n<p>12 A Simultaneous linear</p>\n<p>equations</p>\n<p>13 C Laws of indices</p>\n<p>14 A Degree of a polynomial</p>\n<p>15 C Arithmetic sequence / nth</p>\n<p>term</p>\n<p>16 D Gradient of a straight line</p>\n<p>17 C Pythagoras theorem</p>\n<p>18 A Volume of a cone</p>\n<p>19 B Parallel lines and</p>\n<p>transversal</p>\n<p>20 D Arc length</p>\n<p>21 A Trigonometric ratio (sine)</p>\n<p>22 B Interior angles of polygons</p>\n<h4>23 B Median</h4>\n<p>24 A Combined probability</p>\n<p>25 B Mean from a frequency</p>\n<p>distribution</p>\n<p>[Table data, extracted with row/column structure]</p>\n</section>', 'file_name': 'ilovepdf_merged.pdf', 'rag_thread_id': 'user_64_conv_48_1790745755_f7b34912', 'conversation_id': 48, 'created_at': '2026-09-29T22:22:37.103991', 'updated_at': '2026-09-29T22:22:37.103999', 'is_public': True, 'has_child_version': False, 'parent_lesson_id': None, 'version': 1, 'lesson_id': 'L1790720557102', 'version_number': 1, 'parent_version_id': None, 'original_content': '# QA Lesson 20260929_151749\n\n**Source document:** ilovepdf_merged.pdf\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="2">\n<h2>Page 2</h2>\n<p>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment for Grade IX students</p>\n<p>Based on Grade VIII Mathematics</p>\n<p>Time: 30 minutes | Total Marks: 25</p>\n<h4>Instructions</h4>\n<p>Choose the one best answer for each question. Attempt all questions. Each question carries 1 mark. You</p>\n<p>may use rough paper for working.</p>\n<p>Domain A. Number &amp; Operations and Sets</p>\n<p>1. Which pair contains one rational number and one irrational number?</p>\n<p>A. \u221a25and 0.4</p>\n<p>B. \u221a8and \U0001d70b</p>\n<p>C. 0.121212 …and</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>D. \u221a49and \u221a10</p>\n<p>2. The number</p>\n<p>\U0001d7d3. \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6 …</p>\n<h4>is best described as</h4>\n<p>A. a rational terminating decimal</p>\n<p>B. a rational non-terminating recurring decimal</p>\n<p>C. an irrational number</p>\n<p>D. an integer</p>\n<p>3. What is</p>\n<p>\u221a\U0001d7ce. \U0001d7ce\U0001d7ce\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf ?</p>\n<h4>A. 0.9</h4>\n<h4>B. 0.009</h4>\n<h4>C. 0.09</h4>\n<h4>D. 0.081</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="3">\n<h2>Page 3</h2>\n<p>4. Eight workers can complete a job in 15 days. At the same rate, how many days would 12 workers take?</p>\n<p>A. 10 days</p>\n<p>B. 12 days</p>\n<p>C. 18 days</p>\n<p>D. 22.5 days</p>\n<p>5. A shopkeeper marks an item at Rs 4,000 and gives a 10% discount. The item originally cost the</p>\n<p>shopkeeper Rs 3,000.</p>\n<p>What percentage profit does the shopkeeper make on the cost price?</p>\n<h4>A. 15%</h4>\n<h4>B. 16</h4>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>3 %</p>\n<h4>C. 20%</h4>\n<h4>D. 33</h4>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>6. A set \U0001d469 has 4 elements. How many elements are in its power set \U0001d477(\U0001d469)?</p>\n<h4>A. 8</h4>\n<h4>B. 4</h4>\n<h4>C. 32</h4>\n<h4>D. 16</h4>\n<p>7. Which statement is always true?</p>\n<p>A. The product of two irrational numbers is always irrational.</p>\n<p>B. The square root of every positive integer is irrational.</p>\n<p>C. Every non-terminating decimal is irrational.</p>\n<p>D. The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.</p>\n<h4>Domain B. Algebra</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<h3>8. Solve</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>3\U0001d465 + 4 &gt; 10</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A. \U0001d465 &lt; -2</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d465 &gt; -2</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d465 &lt; 2</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d465 &gt; 2</p>\n<h4>9. Simplify</h4>\n<p>3\U0001d465(2\U0001d465 - 5) - 2\U0001d465(\U0001d465 - 4)</p>\n<p>A. 4\U0001d4652 - 23\U0001d465</p>\n<p>B. 8\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>C. 4\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>D. 4\U0001d4652 + 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>10. Which expression is equal to</p>\n<p>(\U0001d7d1\U0001d499 - \U0001d7d0)\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. 9\U0001d4652 - 4</p>\n<p>B. 9\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>C. 9\U0001d4652 - 6\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>D. 6\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>11. Which is the correct factorization of</p>\n<p>\U0001d499\U0001d7d0 + \U0001d499 - \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. (\U0001d465 - 4)(\U0001d465 + 3)</p>\n<p>B. (\U0001d465 + 6)(\U0001d465 - 2)</p>\n<p>C. (\U0001d465 + 12)(\U0001d465 - 1)</p>\n<p>D. (\U0001d465 + 4)(\U0001d465 - 3)</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>12. Which ordered pair satisfies both equations?</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 + \U0001d466 = 9</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 - \U0001d466 = 3</p>\n<h4>A. (6, 3)</h4>\n<h4>B. (3, 6)</h4>\n<h4>C. (7, 2)</h4>\n<h4>D. (5, 4)</h4>\n<h4>13. Simplify</h4>\n<p>(\U0001d44e3\U0001d44f2)(\U0001d44e2\U0001d44f4)</p>\n<p>\U0001d44e\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>A. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f6</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d44e5\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d44e6\U0001d44f9</p>\n<p>14. What is the degree of the polynomial \U0001d7d2\U0001d499\U0001d7d1\U0001d49a\U0001d7d0 - \U0001d7d5\U0001d499\U0001d49a\U0001d7d2 + \U0001d7d1?</p>\n<h4>A. 5</h4>\n<h4>B. 2</h4>\n<h4>C. 4</h4>\n<h4>D. 8</h4>\n<p>15. The arithmetic sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ... continues in the same way. Which expression gives its nth</p>\n<p>term?</p>\n<p>A. 5n + 7</p>\n<p>B. 7n - 2</p>\n<p>C. 5n + 2</p>\n<p>D. 5n - 2</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="6">\n<h2>Page 6</h2>\n<p>16. A straight line passes through the points (-1, 2) and (3, 10). What is the gradient of the line?</p>\n<h3>A. 1/2</h3>\n<h3>B. 4</h3>\n<h4>C. -2</h4>\n<h4>D. 2</h4>\n<h4>Domain C. Measurement &amp; Geometry</h4>\n<p>17. A ladder 15 m long is placed against a wall. If its upper end reaches the height of 12 m along the wall,</p>\n<p>then find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.</p>\n<p>A. 3 m</p>\n<p>B. 6 m</p>\n<p>C. 9 m</p>\n<p>D. 12 m</p>\n<p>18. A cone has a base area of \U0001d7d1\U0001d7ce cm\U0001d7d0and a perpendicular height of 9 cm.</p>\n<p>What is its volume?</p>\n<p>A. 90 cm3</p>\n<p>B. 270 cm3</p>\n<p>C. 30 cm3</p>\n<p>D. 810 cm3</p>\n<p>19. Choose the correct statement.</p>\n<p>A. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are congruent.</p>\n<p>B. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are supplementary.</p>\n<p>C. The diagonals of every rectangle bisect each other at right angles.</p>\n<p>D. If a transversal intersects any two lines, the alternate interior angles are always congruent.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="7">\n<h2>Page 7</h2>\n<p>20. A circle has radius 7 cm. An arc subtends an angle of \U0001d7d7\U0001d7ce\u2218at the centre.</p>\n<p>Using \U0001d70b =</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>7 , what is the length of the arc?</p>\n<p>A. 5.5 cm</p>\n<p>B. 22 cm</p>\n<p>C. 44 cm</p>\n<p>D. 11 cm</p>\n<p>21. In a right-angled triangle, if the opposite side = 4 cm and the hypotenuse = 5 cm, then \U0001d460\U0001d456\U0001d45b \U0001d703 =?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>22. What is the sum of the interior angles of a 7-sided polygon?</p>\n<h4>A. 720\u2218</h4>\n<h4>B. 900\u2218</h4>\n<h4>C. 1080\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. 1260\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. Statistics &amp; Probability</h4>\n<h4>23. Find the median of</h4>\n<p>12, 4, 7, 2, 5</p>\n<h4>A. 6</h4>\n<h4>B. 5</h4>\n<h4>C. 7</h4>\n<h4>D. 10</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="8">\n<h2>Page 8</h2>\n<p>24. A fair coin is tossed and a fair six-sided die is rolled.</p>\n<p>What is the probability of getting a head and an even number?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>25. The scores 2, 4, 6 and 8 occur with frequencies 1, 3, 4 and 2 respectively. What is the mean score?</p>\n<h4>A. 5.0</h4>\n<h4>B. 5.4</h4>\n<h4>C. 6.0</h4>\n<h4>D. 5.6</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="9">\n<h2>Page 9</h2>\n<h3>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment - Answer Key</h3>\n<p>Grade IX | Based on Grade VIII Mathematics | 25 Marks</p>\n<h3>Question Answer Diagnostic Concept</h3>\n<p>1 D Rational and irrational</p>\n<p>numbers</p>\n<p>2 B Recurring decimals</p>\n<p>3 C Square roots of decimals</p>\n<p>4 A Inverse proportion</p>\n<p>5 C Financial arithmetic / profit</p>\n<p>percentage</p>\n<p>6 D Power set</p>\n<p>7 D Properties of rational and</p>\n<p>irrational numbers</p>\n<p>8 A Linear inequality</p>\n<p>9 C Simplification of algebraic</p>\n<p>expressions</p>\n<p>10 B Algebraic identity</p>\n<h4>11 D Factorization</h4>\n<p>12 A Simultaneous linear</p>\n<p>equations</p>\n<p>13 C Laws of indices</p>\n<p>14 A Degree of a polynomial</p>\n<p>15 C Arithmetic sequence / nth</p>\n<p>term</p>\n<p>16 D Gradient of a straight line</p>\n<p>17 C Pythagoras theorem</p>\n<p>18 A Volume of a cone</p>\n<p>19 B Parallel lines and</p>\n<p>transversal</p>\n<p>20 D Arc length</p>\n<p>21 A Trigonometric ratio (sine)</p>\n<p>22 B Interior angles of polygons</p>\n<h4>23 B Median</h4>\n<p>24 A Combined probability</p>\n<p>25 B Mean from a frequency</p>\n<p>distribution</p>\n<p>[Table data, extracted with row/column structure]</p>\n</section>', 'draft_content': None, 'status': 'finalized', 'teacher_name': 'qa_teacher_151749', 'is_original': True}]
2026-09-29 15:22:37,665 - app.models.models - INFO - Versions data: [{'id': 18, 'teacher_id': 64, 'title': 'QA Lesson 20260929_151749', 'summary': 'QA lesson', 'detailed_answer': None, 'learning_objectives': '', 'focus_area': 'Math', 'grade_level': '7', 'content': '# QA Lesson 20260929_151749\n\n**Source document:** ilovepdf_merged.pdf\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="2">\n<h2>Page 2</h2>\n<p>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment for Grade IX students</p>\n<p>Based on Grade VIII Mathematics</p>\n<p>Time: 30 minutes | Total Marks: 25</p>\n<h4>Instructions</h4>\n<p>Choose the one best answer for each question. Attempt all questions. Each question carries 1 mark. You</p>\n<p>may use rough paper for working.</p>\n<p>Domain A. Number &amp; Operations and Sets</p>\n<p>1. Which pair contains one rational number and one irrational number?</p>\n<p>A. \u221a25and 0.4</p>\n<p>B. \u221a8and \U0001d70b</p>\n<p>C. 0.121212 …and</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>D. \u221a49and \u221a10</p>\n<p>2. The number</p>\n<p>\U0001d7d3. \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6 …</p>\n<h4>is best described as</h4>\n<p>A. a rational terminating decimal</p>\n<p>B. a rational non-terminating recurring decimal</p>\n<p>C. an irrational number</p>\n<p>D. an integer</p>\n<p>3. What is</p>\n<p>\u221a\U0001d7ce. \U0001d7ce\U0001d7ce\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf ?</p>\n<h4>A. 0.9</h4>\n<h4>B. 0.009</h4>\n<h4>C. 0.09</h4>\n<h4>D. 0.081</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="3">\n<h2>Page 3</h2>\n<p>4. Eight workers can complete a job in 15 days. At the same rate, how many days would 12 workers take?</p>\n<p>A. 10 days</p>\n<p>B. 12 days</p>\n<p>C. 18 days</p>\n<p>D. 22.5 days</p>\n<p>5. A shopkeeper marks an item at Rs 4,000 and gives a 10% discount. The item originally cost the</p>\n<p>shopkeeper Rs 3,000.</p>\n<p>What percentage profit does the shopkeeper make on the cost price?</p>\n<h4>A. 15%</h4>\n<h4>B. 16</h4>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>3 %</p>\n<h4>C. 20%</h4>\n<h4>D. 33</h4>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>6. A set \U0001d469 has 4 elements. How many elements are in its power set \U0001d477(\U0001d469)?</p>\n<h4>A. 8</h4>\n<h4>B. 4</h4>\n<h4>C. 32</h4>\n<h4>D. 16</h4>\n<p>7. Which statement is always true?</p>\n<p>A. The product of two irrational numbers is always irrational.</p>\n<p>B. The square root of every positive integer is irrational.</p>\n<p>C. Every non-terminating decimal is irrational.</p>\n<p>D. The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.</p>\n<h4>Domain B. Algebra</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<h3>8. Solve</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>3\U0001d465 + 4 &gt; 10</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A. \U0001d465 &lt; -2</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d465 &gt; -2</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d465 &lt; 2</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d465 &gt; 2</p>\n<h4>9. Simplify</h4>\n<p>3\U0001d465(2\U0001d465 - 5) - 2\U0001d465(\U0001d465 - 4)</p>\n<p>A. 4\U0001d4652 - 23\U0001d465</p>\n<p>B. 8\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>C. 4\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>D. 4\U0001d4652 + 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>10. Which expression is equal to</p>\n<p>(\U0001d7d1\U0001d499 - \U0001d7d0)\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. 9\U0001d4652 - 4</p>\n<p>B. 9\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>C. 9\U0001d4652 - 6\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>D. 6\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>11. Which is the correct factorization of</p>\n<p>\U0001d499\U0001d7d0 + \U0001d499 - \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. (\U0001d465 - 4)(\U0001d465 + 3)</p>\n<p>B. (\U0001d465 + 6)(\U0001d465 - 2)</p>\n<p>C. (\U0001d465 + 12)(\U0001d465 - 1)</p>\n<p>D. (\U0001d465 + 4)(\U0001d465 - 3)</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>12. Which ordered pair satisfies both equations?</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 + \U0001d466 = 9</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 - \U0001d466 = 3</p>\n<h4>A. (6, 3)</h4>\n<h4>B. (3, 6)</h4>\n<h4>C. (7, 2)</h4>\n<h4>D. (5, 4)</h4>\n<h4>13. Simplify</h4>\n<p>(\U0001d44e3\U0001d44f2)(\U0001d44e2\U0001d44f4)</p>\n<p>\U0001d44e\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>A. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f6</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d44e5\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d44e6\U0001d44f9</p>\n<p>14. What is the degree of the polynomial \U0001d7d2\U0001d499\U0001d7d1\U0001d49a\U0001d7d0 - \U0001d7d5\U0001d499\U0001d49a\U0001d7d2 + \U0001d7d1?</p>\n<h4>A. 5</h4>\n<h4>B. 2</h4>\n<h4>C. 4</h4>\n<h4>D. 8</h4>\n<p>15. The arithmetic sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ... continues in the same way. Which expression gives its nth</p>\n<p>term?</p>\n<p>A. 5n + 7</p>\n<p>B. 7n - 2</p>\n<p>C. 5n + 2</p>\n<p>D. 5n - 2</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="6">\n<h2>Page 6</h2>\n<p>16. A straight line passes through the points (-1, 2) and (3, 10). What is the gradient of the line?</p>\n<h3>A. 1/2</h3>\n<h3>B. 4</h3>\n<h4>C. -2</h4>\n<h4>D. 2</h4>\n<h4>Domain C. Measurement &amp; Geometry</h4>\n<p>17. A ladder 15 m long is placed against a wall. If its upper end reaches the height of 12 m along the wall,</p>\n<p>then find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.</p>\n<p>A. 3 m</p>\n<p>B. 6 m</p>\n<p>C. 9 m</p>\n<p>D. 12 m</p>\n<p>18. A cone has a base area of \U0001d7d1\U0001d7ce cm\U0001d7d0and a perpendicular height of 9 cm.</p>\n<p>What is its volume?</p>\n<p>A. 90 cm3</p>\n<p>B. 270 cm3</p>\n<p>C. 30 cm3</p>\n<p>D. 810 cm3</p>\n<p>19. Choose the correct statement.</p>\n<p>A. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are congruent.</p>\n<p>B. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are supplementary.</p>\n<p>C. The diagonals of every rectangle bisect each other at right angles.</p>\n<p>D. If a transversal intersects any two lines, the alternate interior angles are always congruent.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="7">\n<h2>Page 7</h2>\n<p>20. A circle has radius 7 cm. An arc subtends an angle of \U0001d7d7\U0001d7ce\u2218at the centre.</p>\n<p>Using \U0001d70b =</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>7 , what is the length of the arc?</p>\n<p>A. 5.5 cm</p>\n<p>B. 22 cm</p>\n<p>C. 44 cm</p>\n<p>D. 11 cm</p>\n<p>21. In a right-angled triangle, if the opposite side = 4 cm and the hypotenuse = 5 cm, then \U0001d460\U0001d456\U0001d45b \U0001d703 =?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>22. What is the sum of the interior angles of a 7-sided polygon?</p>\n<h4>A. 720\u2218</h4>\n<h4>B. 900\u2218</h4>\n<h4>C. 1080\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. 1260\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. Statistics &amp; Probability</h4>\n<h4>23. Find the median of</h4>\n<p>12, 4, 7, 2, 5</p>\n<h4>A. 6</h4>\n<h4>B. 5</h4>\n<h4>C. 7</h4>\n<h4>D. 10</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="8">\n<h2>Page 8</h2>\n<p>24. A fair coin is tossed and a fair six-sided die is rolled.</p>\n<p>What is the probability of getting a head and an even number?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>25. The scores 2, 4, 6 and 8 occur with frequencies 1, 3, 4 and 2 respectively. What is the mean score?</p>\n<h4>A. 5.0</h4>\n<h4>B. 5.4</h4>\n<h4>C. 6.0</h4>\n<h4>D. 5.6</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="9">\n<h2>Page 9</h2>\n<h3>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment - Answer Key</h3>\n<p>Grade IX | Based on Grade VIII Mathematics | 25 Marks</p>\n<h3>Question Answer Diagnostic Concept</h3>\n<p>1 D Rational and irrational</p>\n<p>numbers</p>\n<p>2 B Recurring decimals</p>\n<p>3 C Square roots of decimals</p>\n<p>4 A Inverse proportion</p>\n<p>5 C Financial arithmetic / profit</p>\n<p>percentage</p>\n<p>6 D Power set</p>\n<p>7 D Properties of rational and</p>\n<p>irrational numbers</p>\n<p>8 A Linear inequality</p>\n<p>9 C Simplification of algebraic</p>\n<p>expressions</p>\n<p>10 B Algebraic identity</p>\n<h4>11 D Factorization</h4>\n<p>12 A Simultaneous linear</p>\n<p>equations</p>\n<p>13 C Laws of indices</p>\n<p>14 A Degree of a polynomial</p>\n<p>15 C Arithmetic sequence / nth</p>\n<p>term</p>\n<p>16 D Gradient of a straight line</p>\n<p>17 C Pythagoras theorem</p>\n<p>18 A Volume of a cone</p>\n<p>19 B Parallel lines and</p>\n<p>transversal</p>\n<p>20 D Arc length</p>\n<p>21 A Trigonometric ratio (sine)</p>\n<p>22 B Interior angles of polygons</p>\n<h4>23 B Median</h4>\n<p>24 A Combined probability</p>\n<p>25 B Mean from a frequency</p>\n<p>distribution</p>\n<p>[Table data, extracted with row/column structure]</p>\n</section>', 'file_name': 'ilovepdf_merged.pdf', 'rag_thread_id': 'user_64_conv_48_1790745755_f7b34912', 'conversation_id': 48, 'created_at': '2026-09-29T22:22:37.103991', 'updated_at': '2026-09-29T22:22:37.103999', 'is_public': True, 'has_child_version': False, 'parent_lesson_id': None, 'version': 1, 'lesson_id': 'L1790720557102', 'version_number': 1, 'parent_version_id': None, 'original_content': '# QA Lesson 20260929_151749\n\n**Source document:** ilovepdf_merged.pdf\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="2">\n<h2>Page 2</h2>\n<p>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment for Grade IX students</p>\n<p>Based on Grade VIII Mathematics</p>\n<p>Time: 30 minutes | Total Marks: 25</p>\n<h4>Instructions</h4>\n<p>Choose the one best answer for each question. Attempt all questions. Each question carries 1 mark. You</p>\n<p>may use rough paper for working.</p>\n<p>Domain A. Number &amp; Operations and Sets</p>\n<p>1. Which pair contains one rational number and one irrational number?</p>\n<p>A. \u221a25and 0.4</p>\n<p>B. \u221a8and \U0001d70b</p>\n<p>C. 0.121212 …and</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>D. \u221a49and \u221a10</p>\n<p>2. The number</p>\n<p>\U0001d7d3. \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf\U0001d7d6 …</p>\n<h4>is best described as</h4>\n<p>A. a rational terminating decimal</p>\n<p>B. a rational non-terminating recurring decimal</p>\n<p>C. an irrational number</p>\n<p>D. an integer</p>\n<p>3. What is</p>\n<p>\u221a\U0001d7ce. \U0001d7ce\U0001d7ce\U0001d7d6\U0001d7cf ?</p>\n<h4>A. 0.9</h4>\n<h4>B. 0.009</h4>\n<h4>C. 0.09</h4>\n<h4>D. 0.081</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="3">\n<h2>Page 3</h2>\n<p>4. Eight workers can complete a job in 15 days. At the same rate, how many days would 12 workers take?</p>\n<p>A. 10 days</p>\n<p>B. 12 days</p>\n<p>C. 18 days</p>\n<p>D. 22.5 days</p>\n<p>5. A shopkeeper marks an item at Rs 4,000 and gives a 10% discount. The item originally cost the</p>\n<p>shopkeeper Rs 3,000.</p>\n<p>What percentage profit does the shopkeeper make on the cost price?</p>\n<h4>A. 15%</h4>\n<h4>B. 16</h4>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>3 %</p>\n<h4>C. 20%</h4>\n<h4>D. 33</h4>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>6. A set \U0001d469 has 4 elements. How many elements are in its power set \U0001d477(\U0001d469)?</p>\n<h4>A. 8</h4>\n<h4>B. 4</h4>\n<h4>C. 32</h4>\n<h4>D. 16</h4>\n<p>7. Which statement is always true?</p>\n<p>A. The product of two irrational numbers is always irrational.</p>\n<p>B. The square root of every positive integer is irrational.</p>\n<p>C. Every non-terminating decimal is irrational.</p>\n<p>D. The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.</p>\n<h4>Domain B. Algebra</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<h3>8. Solve</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>3\U0001d465 + 4 &gt; 10</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A. \U0001d465 &lt; -2</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d465 &gt; -2</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d465 &lt; 2</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d465 &gt; 2</p>\n<h4>9. Simplify</h4>\n<p>3\U0001d465(2\U0001d465 - 5) - 2\U0001d465(\U0001d465 - 4)</p>\n<p>A. 4\U0001d4652 - 23\U0001d465</p>\n<p>B. 8\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>C. 4\U0001d4652 - 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>D. 4\U0001d4652 + 7\U0001d465</p>\n<p>10. Which expression is equal to</p>\n<p>(\U0001d7d1\U0001d499 - \U0001d7d0)\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. 9\U0001d4652 - 4</p>\n<p>B. 9\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>C. 9\U0001d4652 - 6\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>D. 6\U0001d4652 - 12\U0001d465 + 4</p>\n<p>11. Which is the correct factorization of</p>\n<p>\U0001d499\U0001d7d0 + \U0001d499 - \U0001d7cf\U0001d7d0?</p>\n<p>A. (\U0001d465 - 4)(\U0001d465 + 3)</p>\n<p>B. (\U0001d465 + 6)(\U0001d465 - 2)</p>\n<p>C. (\U0001d465 + 12)(\U0001d465 - 1)</p>\n<p>D. (\U0001d465 + 4)(\U0001d465 - 3)</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>12. Which ordered pair satisfies both equations?</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 + \U0001d466 = 9</p>\n<p>\U0001d465 - \U0001d466 = 3</p>\n<h4>A. (6, 3)</h4>\n<h4>B. (3, 6)</h4>\n<h4>C. (7, 2)</h4>\n<h4>D. (5, 4)</h4>\n<h4>13. Simplify</h4>\n<p>(\U0001d44e3\U0001d44f2)(\U0001d44e2\U0001d44f4)</p>\n<p>\U0001d44e\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>A. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f6</p>\n<p>B. \U0001d44e5\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>C. \U0001d44e4\U0001d44f3</p>\n<p>D. \U0001d44e6\U0001d44f9</p>\n<p>14. What is the degree of the polynomial \U0001d7d2\U0001d499\U0001d7d1\U0001d49a\U0001d7d0 - \U0001d7d5\U0001d499\U0001d49a\U0001d7d2 + \U0001d7d1?</p>\n<h4>A. 5</h4>\n<h4>B. 2</h4>\n<h4>C. 4</h4>\n<h4>D. 8</h4>\n<p>15. The arithmetic sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ... continues in the same way. Which expression gives its nth</p>\n<p>term?</p>\n<p>A. 5n + 7</p>\n<p>B. 7n - 2</p>\n<p>C. 5n + 2</p>\n<p>D. 5n - 2</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="6">\n<h2>Page 6</h2>\n<p>16. A straight line passes through the points (-1, 2) and (3, 10). What is the gradient of the line?</p>\n<h3>A. 1/2</h3>\n<h3>B. 4</h3>\n<h4>C. -2</h4>\n<h4>D. 2</h4>\n<h4>Domain C. Measurement &amp; Geometry</h4>\n<p>17. A ladder 15 m long is placed against a wall. If its upper end reaches the height of 12 m along the wall,</p>\n<p>then find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.</p>\n<p>A. 3 m</p>\n<p>B. 6 m</p>\n<p>C. 9 m</p>\n<p>D. 12 m</p>\n<p>18. A cone has a base area of \U0001d7d1\U0001d7ce cm\U0001d7d0and a perpendicular height of 9 cm.</p>\n<p>What is its volume?</p>\n<p>A. 90 cm3</p>\n<p>B. 270 cm3</p>\n<p>C. 30 cm3</p>\n<p>D. 810 cm3</p>\n<p>19. Choose the correct statement.</p>\n<p>A. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are congruent.</p>\n<p>B. If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles on the same side of the</p>\n<p>transversal are supplementary.</p>\n<p>C. The diagonals of every rectangle bisect each other at right angles.</p>\n<p>D. If a transversal intersects any two lines, the alternate interior angles are always congruent.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="7">\n<h2>Page 7</h2>\n<p>20. A circle has radius 7 cm. An arc subtends an angle of \U0001d7d7\U0001d7ce\u2218at the centre.</p>\n<p>Using \U0001d70b =</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>7 , what is the length of the arc?</p>\n<p>A. 5.5 cm</p>\n<p>B. 22 cm</p>\n<p>C. 44 cm</p>\n<p>D. 11 cm</p>\n<p>21. In a right-angled triangle, if the opposite side = 4 cm and the hypotenuse = 5 cm, then \U0001d460\U0001d456\U0001d45b \U0001d703 =?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>22. What is the sum of the interior angles of a 7-sided polygon?</p>\n<h4>A. 720\u2218</h4>\n<h4>B. 900\u2218</h4>\n<h4>C. 1080\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. 1260\u2218</h4>\n<h4>D. Statistics &amp; Probability</h4>\n<h4>23. Find the median of</h4>\n<p>12, 4, 7, 2, 5</p>\n<h4>A. 6</h4>\n<h4>B. 5</h4>\n<h4>C. 7</h4>\n<h4>D. 10</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="8">\n<h2>Page 8</h2>\n<p>24. A fair coin is tossed and a fair six-sided die is rolled.</p>\n<p>What is the probability of getting a head and an even number?</p>\n<p>A.</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>B.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>C.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>D.</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>25. The scores 2, 4, 6 and 8 occur with frequencies 1, 3, 4 and 2 respectively. What is the mean score?</p>\n<h4>A. 5.0</h4>\n<h4>B. 5.4</h4>\n<h4>C. 6.0</h4>\n<h4>D. 5.6</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="9">\n<h2>Page 9</h2>\n<h3>Mathematics Diagnostic Baseline Assessment - Answer Key</h3>\n<p>Grade IX | Based on Grade VIII Mathematics | 25 Marks</p>\n<h3>Question Answer Diagnostic Concept</h3>\n<p>1 D Rational and irrational</p>\n<p>numbers</p>\n<p>2 B Recurring decimals</p>\n<p>3 C Square roots of decimals</p>\n<p>4 A Inverse proportion</p>\n<p>5 C Financial arithmetic / profit</p>\n<p>percentage</p>\n<p>6 D Power set</p>\n<p>7 D Properties of rational and</p>\n<p>irrational numbers</p>\n<p>8 A Linear inequality</p>\n<p>9 C Simplification of algebraic</p>\n<p>expressions</p>\n<p>10 B Algebraic identity</p>\n<h4>11 D Factorization</h4>\n<p>12 A Simultaneous linear</p>\n<p>equations</p>\n<p>13 C Laws of indices</p>\n<p>14 A Degree of a polynomial</p>\n<p>15 C Arithmetic sequence / nth</p>\n<p>term</p>\n<p>16 D Gradient of a straight line</p>\n<p>17 C Pythagoras theorem</p>\n<p>18 A Volume of a cone</p>\n<p>19 B Parallel lines and</p>\n<p>transversal</p>\n<p>20 D Arc length</p>\n<p>21 A Trigonometric ratio (sine)</p>\n<p>22 B Interior angles of polygons</p>\n<h4>23 B Median</h4>\n<p>24 A Combined probability</p>\n<p>25 B Mean from a frequency</p>\n<p>distribution</p>\n<p>[Table data, extracted with row/column structure]</p>\n</section>', 'draft_content': None, 'status': 'finalized', 'teacher_name': 'qa_teacher_151749', 'is_original': True}]
